'''''Cotoneaster glaucophyllus''''', commonly known as '''glaucous cotoneaster''''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=17 October 2014}}</ref> or '''bright bead cotoneaster''', is a [[native plant|native]] plant of [[China]] and the [[Himalayas]].<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Cotoneaster+glaucophyllus|title=''Cotoneaster glaucophyllus''|publisher=[[Plants for a Future|PFAF]]|access-date=9 April 2013}}</ref>

''Cotoneaster glaucophyllus'' is a spreading evergreen shrub growing up to {{convert|5|m}} tall. The oblong leaves are {{convert|1.5|–|4|cm}} wide by {{convert|3|–|8|cm}} long, with hairy undersides when young. Clumps of red berries are produced after flowering.<ref name=terrain>{{cite web|title=Cotoneaster|url=http://www.terrain.net.nz/friends-of-te-henui-group/weeds/cotoneaster-franchetii.html|publisher=Taranaki Educational Resource: Research, Analysis and Information Network|access-date=2 December 2012}}</ref>

In [[Australia]] and [[New Zealand]] it is considered a weed.

==Description==
The plant is {{convert|4|m}} tall with arched and erect branches which are greenish to purple-black in colour. Fertile shoots are {{convert|40|-|70|mm}} long including two to four leaves. Its [[Pedicel (botany)|pedicel]]s are {{convert|2|-|6|mm}} long and are strigose. The leaves are dull to somewhat shiny and mid-green in colour with light green undersides. Flower buds are white, also the flowers, with a [[Corolla (flower)|corolla]] {{convert|5|-|6|mm}} wide. Fruits are globose, orange, and {{convert|6|mm}} wide.<ref>{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wTsHReVAUroC&q=Cotoneaster+glaucophyllus&pg=PA49|author=[[Jeanette Fryer]] & [[Bertil Hylmö]]|title=Cotoneasters: a comprehensive guide to shrubs for flowers, fruit, and foliage|year=2009|page=49|isbn=9780881929270}}</ref>

